nt DIM_X, const int DIM_Y, const int TILE_SIZE, magma_trans_t trans>\nL546: \nL547: __global__ void\nL548: \nL549: gemvc_kernel_batched(\nL550: \nL551: int m, int n, T alpha,\nL552: T const * const * A_array, T const * A, int lda, int strideA, int Ai, int Aj,\nL553: \nL554: T const * const * x_array, T const * x, int incx, int stridex, int xi,\nL555: \nL556: T beta, T** y_array, T * y, int incy, int stridey, int yi)\nL557: \nL558: {\nL559: \nL560: int batchid = blockIdx.z;\nL561: \nL562: const T* dA = (A_array == NULL) ? (A + batchid * strideA + Aj * lda + Ai) : ( A_array[batchid] + Aj * lda + Ai );\nL563: \nL564: const T* dx = (x_array == NULL) ? (x + batchid * stridex + xi * incx) : ( x_array[batchid] + xi * incx );\nL565: T* dy = (y_array == NULL) ? (y + batchid * stridey + yi * incy) : ( y_array[batchid] + yi * incy );\nL566: \nL567: gemvc_template_device<T, DIM_X, DIM_Y, TILE_SIZE, trans>\nL568: \nL569: (m, n, alpha, dA, lda, dx, incx, beta, dy, incy);\nL570: \nL571: }\nL572: \nL573: /******************************************************************************/\nL574: \nL575: template <typename T, const int DIM_X, const int DIM_Y, const int TILE_SIZE>\nL576: \nL577: void gemvc_template_batched(\nL578: \nL579: magma_trans_t trans, magma_int_t m, magma_int_t n, T alpha,\nL580: T const * const * dA_array, T const * dA, magma_int_t ldda, magma_int_t strideA, magma_int_t Ai, magma_int_t Aj,\nL581: \nL582: T const * const * dx_array, T const * dx, magma_int_t incx, magma_int_t stridex, magma_int_t xi,\nL583: \nL584: T beta, T** dy_array, T * dy, magma_int_t incy, magma_int_t stridey, magma_int_t yi,\nL585: \nL586: magma_int_t batchCount, magma_queue_t queue)\nL587: \nL588: {\nL589: \nL590: magma_int_t max_batchCount = queue->get_maxBatch();\nL591: \nL592: dim3 threads ( DIM_X, DIM_Y );\nL593: \nL594: for(magma_int_t i=0; i<batchCount; i+=max_batchCount) {\nL595: magma_int_t ibatch = min(max_batchCount, batchCount-i);\nL596: \nL597: dim3 grid( magma_ceildiv(n, TILE_SIZE), 1, ibatch );\nL598: \nL599: T const * const * dA_array_i = (dA_array == NULL) ? NULL : dA_array+i;\nL600: \nL601: T const * const * dx_array_i = (dx_array == NULL) ? NULL : dx_array+i;\nL602: \nL603: T** dy_array_i = (dy_array == NULL) ? NULL : dy_array+i;\nL604: \nL605: if (trans == MagmaConjTrans) {\nL606: \nL607: gemvc_kernel_batched<T, DIM_X, DIM_Y, TILE_SIZE, MagmaConjTrans>\nL608: \nL609: <<< grid, threads, 0, queue->cuda_stream() >>>\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGPIR: Enabling Practical Private Information Retrieval with GPUs (https://arxiv.org/html/2604.04696v2)\nciteturn10view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"lineno\":213}); Total lines: 455\nL208: #### 4.1.2. Stage-level kernels\nL209: \nL210: In addition to the baseline operation-level kernels, we introduce stage-level kernels that process an entire stage of ExpandQuery/ColTor within a single kernel invocation. A stage-level kernel fuses all operation-level kernels within a stage of ExpandQuery/ColTor, such as NTT, $\\mathrm{Dcp}$, and multiplications with $\\mathtt{evk}$s or RGSW ciphertexts, into a single kernel.\nL211: Through kernel fusion, stage-level kernels retain intermediate results—specifically the transient polynomials produced during $\\mathrm{Dcp}$—within SM registers or shared memory. This localized data handling effectively avoids hitting the cache-capacity wall, alleviating the performance bottlenecks otherwise caused by the limited L2 cache capacity and working-set spikes.\nL212: However, stage-level kernels reduce parallelism, which can adversely impact performance. Operation-level kernels exploit not only ciphertext-wise partitioning but also limb-wise and coefficient-wise partitioning to distribute work across thread blocks.\nL213: However, fusing these operation-level kernels into a single stage-level kernel introduces interleaved partitioning patterns that create all-to-all data dependencies within each $\\mathtt{ct}$, requiring each thread block to process an entire $\\mathtt{ct}$ (i.e., a single tree node). As a result, stage-level kernels substantially reduce the number of thread blocks, which can lower occupancy and degrade performance.\nL214: Multi-client batching increases the number of $\\mathtt{ct}$s and makes stage-level kernels increasingly viable, as ciphertext-wise partitioning alone can provide sufficient parallelism to fully utilize GPU resources. The ExpandQuery/ColTor tree structure exposes two sources of ciphertext-wise data parallelism: (1) parallel processing of multiple nodes at the same stage (tree depth) and (2) parallelism across multiple $\\mathtt{ct}$s introduced by multi-client batching.\nL215: While only dozens of thread blocks can be populated at shallow tree depths with stage-level kernels, up to tens of thousands of thread blocks can be populated for deeper tree depths.\nL216: (a) DRAM transaction (ExpandQuery)\nL217: \nL218: (b) DRAM transaction (ColTor)\nL219: \nL220: (c) Execution time (ExpandQuery)\nL221: \nL222: (d) Execution time (ColTor)\nL223: \nL224: Figure 5. Stage-wise DRAM transaction and execution time of operation- and stage-level kernels under various batch sizes. (a, b) DRAM transactions per query per node and (c, d) Amortized execution time per query for ExpandQuery/ColTor.\nL225: ### 4.2. Stage-Wise Comparison of Kernel Behaviors\nL226: \nL227: To quantitatively analyze the trade-offs between operation-level and stage-level kernel designs, we profile DRAM traffic and execution time for each stage. We focus primarily on the memory behavior as both ExpandQuery and ColTor are constrained by DRAM bandwidth under batching (see Fig. 【92†3】). Our analysis uses NVIDIA Nsight Compute (【94†NVIDIA Corporation, 2025a】) on an NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPU.\nL228: #### 4.2.1. DRAM traffic trends\nL229: Fig. 【95†5a】 and Fig. 【96†5b】 show that DRAM traffic exhibits strong stage dependence under batching, and that the two kernel designs respond differently to working-set growth. For early stages in ExpandQuery and later stages in ColTor, where the number of $\\mathtt{ct}$s is small, the working set fits within the L2 cache (see Fig. 【93†4】), resulting in low DRAM traffic for both operation-level and stage-level kernel designs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen Proofs Meet Hardware: Comparing NTT and SumCheck in Zero-Knowledge Systems (https://arxiv.org/html/2606.16146v1)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view1\",\"lineno\":256}); Total lines: 427\nL252: The RTL is synthesized with Synopsys Design Compiler against a TSMC 22 nm technology library, and SRAM capacity is estimated using a Synopsys 22 nm Memory Compiler. The results are scaled to 7 nm advanced process nodes using standard scaling factors of $3.6\\times$ for area and $3.3\\times$ for power (【74†Daftardar et al., 2024】; 【57†Daftardar et al., 2025b】), with a target frequency of 1 GHz.\nL253: ### 5.2. Impact of On-Chip SRAM Availability\nL254: We start our analysis of NTT and SumCheck by categorizing workloads based on how much of the computation can fit into on-chip SRAM.\nL255: We study three representative cases: (1) small workloads, where sufficient SRAM allows the entire workload to be kept on-chip and executed in parallel; (2) medium workloads, where SRAM can only hold one polynomial at a time to compute its NTT, and execution proceeds in a streaming fashion; and (3) large workloads, where even a full NTT does not fit and must be decomposed into smaller steps.\nL256: SumCheck exhibits similar behavior: with ample SRAM, it runs with all MLEs entirely on-chip (Full on-chip mode), taking minimal data movement, while with more limited SRAM it operates in a streaming mode, repeatedly loading MLEs from off-chip memory, performing updates, and writing results back.\nL257: Across these three cases, we evaluate a range of workloads and highlight the performance trade-offs between NTT- and SumCheck-based hardware, showing how system behavior transitions under different hardware constraints. To ensure fair comparison, we filter results to the Pareto front, where each design point represents a balance between hardware cost (area) and performance (runtime).\nL258: By focusing on Pareto-optimal points for both SumCheck and NTT across all potential designs, we capture systems operating at high resource utilization, avoiding under-provisioned or inefficient configurations.\nL259: #### 5.2.1. Scenario I–Small Workloads\nL260: \nL261: Figure 8. Pareto Frontiers for small workloads scenario, with increasing number of unique polynomial terms. At large design points, NTT has better performance than SumCheck.\nL262: We begin with the case where the workload is small enough to fit entirely on-chip. 【88†Figure 8】 and 【89†Figure 9】 show the Pareto space analysis for two groups of polynomial workloads: one where the number of additive terms increases, and one where the polynomial degree (i.e., multiplicative terms) increases.\nL263: For example, for a workload $f=g_{1}g_{2}$ at size $N=2^{17}$, an on-chip SRAM budget of 18 MB is sufficient to store both $g_{1}$ and $g_{2}$ entirely on-chip and to execute their NTTs in parallel, without incurring off-chip load and write-back traffic. Similarly, SumCheck can perform each round within the SRAM buffers, with updated multilinear extensions (MLEs) written back into the same buffer.\nL264: This requirement is illustrated in 【90†Figure 10】 (a), which plots the SRAM needed for representative polynomials. In this example, 18 MB is enough to support both on-chip NTT and SumCheck.\nL265: Figure 9. Pareto Frontiers for small workloads scenario, with increasing polynomial degrees. As the degree grows, the gap between SumCheck and NTT becomes larger.\nL266: Figure 10. Memory requirement in different scenarios. If the available memory does not reach the requirement (surface in the plot), the system cannot support the corresponding execution. (a) To fit all polynomials of a small workload full on-chip. (b) Consider partially streaming NTT. (c) Consider fully streaming (four-step) NTT. The memory requirement for full on-chip SumCheck at this large workload is theoretical.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. NVIDIA Blackwell Tuning Guide — Blackwell Tuning Guide 13.4 documentation (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/blackwell-tuning-guide/index.html)\nciteturn10view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/blackwell-tuning-guide/index.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 121\nL0: [Input: Search docs] [Input] [Input]\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Blackwell Tuning Guide】\nL3: \nL4: Tuning CUDA Applications for Blackwell GPU Architecture\nL5: \nL6: The programming guide for tuning CUDA Applications for GPUs based on the Blackwell GPU Architecture.\nL7: \nL8: # 1. NVIDIA Blackwell Tuning Guide【1†】\nL9: ## 1.1. NVIDIA Blackwell GPU Architecture【2†】\nL10: The NVIDIA® Blackwell GPU architecture is NVIDIA’s latest architecture for CUDA® compute applications. The NVIDIA Blackwell GPU architecture retains and extends the same CUDA programming model provided by previous NVIDIA GPU architectures such as NVIDIA Ampere GPU architecture and NVIDIA Hopper. Applications that follow the best practices for those architectures should typically see speedups on the Blackwell GPUs without any code changes.\nL11: This guide summarizes the ways that an application can be fine-tuned to gain additional speedups by leveraging the NVIDIA Blackwell GPU architecture’s features.【3†1】\nL12: For further details on the programming features discussed in this guide, refer to the 【4†CUDA Programming Guide】.\nL13: ## 1.2. CUDA Best Practices【5†】\nL14: \nL15: The performance guidelines and best practices described in the 【4†CUDA Programming Guide】 and the 【6†CUDA C++ Best Practices Guide】 apply to all CUDA-capable GPU architectures. Programmers must primarily focus on following those recommendations to achieve the best performance.\nL16: \nL17: The high-priority recommendations from those guides are as follows:\nL18: \nL19:   * Find ways to parallelize sequential code.\nL20: \nL21:   * Minimize data transfers between the host and the device.\nL22:   * Adjust kernel launch configuration to maximize device utilization.\nL23: \nL24:   * Ensure that global memory accesses are coalesced.\nL25: \nL26:   * Minimize redundant accesses to global memory whenever possible.\nL27: \nL28:   * Avoid long sequences of diverged execution by threads within the same warp.\nL29: ## 1.3. Application Compatibility【7†】\nL30: \nL31: Before addressing specific performance tuning issues covered in this guide, refer to the 【8†Blackwell Compatibility Guide for CUDA Applications】 to ensure that your application is compiled in a way that is compatible with NVIDIA Blackwell.\nL32: \nL33: ## 1.4. NVIDIA Blackwell Tuning【9†】\nL34: \nL35: ### 1.4.1. Streaming Multiprocessor【10†】\nL36: \nL37: The NVIDIA Blackwell Streaming Multiprocessor (SM) provides the following improvements over the NVIDIA Hopper GPU architecture.\n"
